  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nA fairly typical mid summer upper level pattern with 598 dam \nridge over the desert southwest, with downstream troughing over \nthe northeast CONUS. Generally west to northwest flow aloft for \nthe Great Lakes, with several embedded shortwaves producing at \nleast scattered shower and storm chances. Gulf moisture is \ngenerally cut off from the upper flow over the northern CONUS, \nwhich will likely limit precipitation coverage over the weekend.\nA more robust shortwave with deeper Pacific moisture, will \nbring much better precipitation chances early next week. \nTemperatures will be on the way up, as thermal ridging builds to\nour west. \n\nA weak shortwave trough and attendant surface reflection begins\nto slide out of Lake Superior and western upper late tonight, \nspreading more clouds into northern parts of the area late. Not \nas cool as the last few nights, with still fairly comfortable \nlows in the 50s to lower 60s (warmest near the coasts and parts \nof upper Michigan). PWATs increase on Saturday as moisture pools\naround the weak surface front, with scattered diurnal \nshower/storm chances. This front washes out across the area \nSaturday night, as the main forcing remains rooted to the west. \nSome hints of a gradually eastward expanding EML plume on Sunday\nas a stronger shortwave slides over the top of the ridge to our\nwest. We remain fairly capped across the Great Lakes and it \nremains to be seen how much diurnal activity will occur. \n\nThe much better chance for showers and storms comes Sunday night \ninto Monday night as the aforementioned more robust wave moves into \nthe Great Lakes.  On Monday, PWAT's surge toward 200% of normal, \ncombined with a warm low level airmass and favorable upper \nlevel kinematics in the exit region of a jet streak. Organized \nstorms possible over parts of the Great Lakes with damaging \nwinds being the main threat. \n\nQuieter weather returns through midweek as surface high pressure \nmoves into the upper Midwest and Great Lakes.",
